We are given a 25‐year‐old man with an itchy rash on his feet and no significant past medical history. Among the choices, Sézary syndrome and mycosis fungoides are types of cutaneous T‐cell lymphomas that typically occur later in life and present with more widespread and often more chronic skin changes along with systemic signs—making them unlikely in this case. Although dyshidrosis (also known as pompholyx) is a type of eczema that can produce itchy vesicular eruptions on the palms and sides of the fingers (and on the feet in rare cases), the clinical picture is more consistent with a localized reaction. In this context, “shoe dermatitis” (a localized form of irritant or allergic contact dermatitis often due to shoe materials, friction, or moisture) is the most common occurrence in a young man with an itchy foot rash. 

