The presentation of asymptomatic, confluent, brown hyperpigmented macules on both the upper and lower lips along with additional macules on the hands is characteristic of Laugier-Hunziker syndrome. This benign condition typically occurs in older adults and is distinguished by these specific patterns of pigmentation without significant associated systemic symptoms. Although Peutz-Jeghers syndrome also involves mucocutaneous pigmentation on the lips and sometimes on the hands, it generally presents earlier in life and is associated with gastrointestinal polyps. The other syndromes listed (Bandler syndrome and McCune-Albright syndrome) do not match this cutaneous presentation.

